Data model?

A data model in an SQL database is the complete, provable contract that defines what every piece of data means, how pieces are related, 
and which rules the database engine must enforce forever — expressed using the relational model so the engine can guarantee correctness and speed automatically.
define your own data model in plain English (this is the real data model)
Imagine a tiny online store. Write the rules in your own words (I already wrote them here so you can follow):

A Customer has: a unique ID, a name, an email, and a creation date.
An Order belongs to exactly one Customer.
An Order has: a unique ID, the date it was placed, and the total amount.
No Order can exist without a Customer (you cannot have an orphan order).
One Customer can have many Orders (but each Order has only one Customer).

This plain-English description = your data model. It lives in your head. It is not SQL yet.
Part B: Now translate it into the relational model (this is what SQL understands)
We express the exact same rules using only the relational model’s building blocks: tables, columns, primary keys, foreign keys, and constraints.
Here is the SQL you can copy-paste and run right now (works in SQLite, MySQL, PostgreSQL — any SQL database):
SQL-- 1. Customers table (a "relation")
CREATE TABLE Customers (
    customer_id   INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,     -- unique identifier
    name          TEXT NOT NULL,            -- must have a name
    email         TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,     -- must be unique
    created_at    DATE NOT NULL             -- must have date
);

-- 2. Orders table (another "relation")
CREATE TABLE Orders (
    order_id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,       -- unique identifier
    customer_id  INTEGER NOT NULL,           -- must belong to a customer
    order_date   DATE NOT NULL,
    total_amount DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,     -- money amount
    FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES Customers(customer_id)
    -- This foreign key is the exact translation of "must belong to exactly one customer"
);
That’s it. Two tables. This is not your data model — this is your data model expressed using the relational model so the SQL engine can understand and protect it.
